UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 18, Lesson 1: Including others', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA game needs fast running and one player cannot run quickly. Write two ways to change the game so all players can take part.\n\nFEEDBACK\nIdeas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is better than leaving someone out.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan an inclusion week at school: write five actions you could do to welcome others (inviting, learning names, adapting games). For each, say who benefits and how you would respond if someone says ''no thanks''.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan an inclusion week at school: write five actions you could do to welcome others (inviting, learning names, adapting games). For each, say who benefits and how you would respond if someone says ''no thanks''. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and tells the group the new student''s name. When the student says they would rather watch today, Ben says ''That''s fine, you can join any time.''\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Correction: Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is better than leaving someone out.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro"