appen in a different place or with different people. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a sh\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named cues, rules or choices.","question":"Describe or practise a fresh situation that uses the central skill. Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and tells the group the new student''s name. When the student says they would rather watch today, Ben says ''That''s fine, you can join any time.''","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the choice or strategy, not just make the answer longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus a corrected choice and a reason linked to safety, health or respect.","question":"Name one likely misconception or unsafe choice for this topic, correct it and say why the correction is safer, healthier or more respectful.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Correction: Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is better than leaving someone out.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w18-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise including others.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use including others with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify belonging and explain why it matters.","I can practise adapting a game independently on a new prompt.","I can notice inviting kindly needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and tells the group the new student''s name. When the student says they would rather watch today, Ben says ''That''s fine, you can join any time.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nA game needs fast running and one player cannot run quickly. Write two ways to change the game so all players can take part.\n\nFEEDBACK\nIdeas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is better than leaving someone out.","explain":"Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Inviting and respecting choice.","caption":"Invite, never force.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\"